frame.initial_lean:
Hold everything, and change nothing on the board:
- fps: hold;
- fps_engine_code: null;
- rate_737: hold;
- re787: hold.
Every order is a default, so nothing goes to the Board as a change.

**Why hold.** This is the Do Nothing row: +0.66 / +0.57.
- The Re-engine adds +0.05 in S1, well inside ε. Doing less and doing it better wins [BX-1239], and so does doctrine's rule that a Re-engine needs a gain above ε.
- In S2 it loses $1.00B, and hold has the higher worst column (+0.57 against -0.43). It is the higher-confidence plan [BX-0548].
- It would put the 787 on one engine source.
- This is the last decision year, so there is no option value in waiting. I close the widebody question for the game and grow the 787 out of Charleston.

**Expected column: S1, provisionally.** Airbus held the A350 Re-engine in 2045 and said so publicly. It gates any further A350 step on technology, engine and business case, and Rolls-Royce says it has nothing to launch. My Round 3 read of S2 was wrong, and I'll own that [BX-1319]. My evidence on Airbus is Low, and Malave gives me his read.
- In S1, holding costs $0.05B, logged as doctrine premium under H8. That brings the running total to at most $1.46B, inside the cap.
- If S2, the premium is $0.
- The order is the same in either column. This is not an answer to Airbus; the business case decides.

**Off the board:**
- a KPI-gated fps ramp;
- durability reviews of both engine sources for the Aerospace Safety Committee;
- supplier health, engines first;
- Charleston growth;
- the capital-return proposal brought back for the Board's reserved decision, sized on the stress case, with the rating confirmation left to the agencies.

**Public statement.** No new dates, no word on Airbus, nothing on returns before the Board decides, and no blame on anyone. 'I think that we're turning it. I don't think it's turned.' [BX-1300] 'the financials will follow our production performance' [BX-1287].

**What would change my lean:**
- a grid gain above ε with no column below -$1B (there is none on this brief);
- a live problem from Pope, which would make me hold harder and pull the buyback first;
- a stress-path breach from Malave, which would shrink the return proposal, not the orders.

