tests.numbers:
**Proposal, in the Board's form:**
- a common dividend of about $3B a year, covered 5.75x by stress FCF of $17.23B and 7.3x by base FCF of $21.83B;
- a buyback of $0-5B, authorised by the Board each year;
- a combined cap of $8B, which is 46% of stress FCF and 37% of base.

**Stress-case net-debt path, 2026 $B, year end.** It assumes the full $8B is paid every year, starts from the tracker's $0.0B at end-2049, and applies the tracker's 2.2% erosion:
- A: -9.24 / -18.27 / -27.11 / -35.77 / -44.23 for 2050-54;
- with a Re-engine: -8.76 / -17.10 / -25.06 / -32.69 / -40.43.

**Liquidity retained.** About $9.24B a year under stress (A), or $8.17-8.76B with a Re-engine. That builds about $44B of net cash by end-2054. The $10B of revolvers stays undrawn and no new equity is needed. The brief carries no gross cash figure, so I show liquidity as net cash built.

**Breakpoints:**
- The buyback goes to zero first on any live quality, FAA or supply problem, including a durability finding on the Joint Venture engine. It also goes to zero if FCF runs below the $8B cap, which is 54% below stress or 63% below base.
- The dividend's floor is FCF covering it once. It holds unless FCF falls 83% below stress (86% below base), and below that only while net debt stays at or below $0.

**Rating.** Our files carry no rating or rating thresholds. The last agency view on our record is Baa3 with a negative outlook (January 2025). The metrics to bring the agencies are:
- net debt at $0 since 2029 (base) and 2030 (stress), against a 2010-18 average of about -$0.6B;
- stress FCF of $17.23B;
- no debt added by the game;
- the pre-crisis comparators of 0.37x net debt to EBITDA and 25.2x interest cover in 2018.
The board still carries $45B of gross debt in its penalty, and the agencies will weigh gross debt too. Last round's 'solidly investment grade' was our inference, and I withdraw it.

**Condition on the decision:** the package takes effect only on the agencies' written confirmation. If they confirm investment grade but not a solid one, I would recommend the dividend only. Nothing is announced before the Board decides.

Boeing narrowbody deliveries on the brief:
- 760 in 2040 (63.3 a month);
- 860 in 2045 (71.7 a month);
- 940 in 2049 (78.3 a month);
- 940 a year, flat, from 2049 to 2060.

That is +15 a month over 9 years: three steps of 5, where doctrine's fastest tempo would allow 18. Since 2049 the rate has held flat at 78.3 a month, and the brief shows no further step to 2060. Demand on the board is 2,000 a year at a flat 47% share, so no step above 78 is called for.

Caveat: 78 a month is Boeing's narrowbody total. The brief gives no split between the 737 MAX and the fps.

The brief gives none of the six KPIs as a number and flags none. It has no in-service reliability data and reports no in-service event. I table the KPI values and reliability at every Aerospace Safety Committee meeting, and I invent no figures. Any further step stays gated on the data, not the calendar.

ops.would_change_mind_if:
Three things would change my results.

(1) On the Re-engine, all of these together:
- a gain above ε ($1B) in the expected column (this grid gives +0.05);
- no column below -$1B;
- a second widebody engine source ready by a 2055 EIS, such as a public Rolls-Royce commitment;
- a date with headroom against our +1 to +4-year slip prior.
This brief offers none of them, and 2050 is the last decision year.

(2) A live quality, FAA, certification or supply problem in the brief, a flagged supplier-shortages KPI, or a durability or life-limited-part finding on the Joint Venture engine. None of these would change the all-hold orders. Each would:
- go to the Aerospace Safety Committee and the Board as a live problem the day we see it;
- send the buyback to zero first;
- move supplier funding up the ladder;
- open a third-source case outside the board, with CFM as the bridge (up to 1,880 fps engines a year).

(3) Malave's stress path showing net debt above $0 at any checkpoint. That would shrink the capital-return proposal, not my orders.

other_moves.detail:
We keep these funded and hot:
- CFM on the fps;
- the Joint Venture engine;
- LEAP-1B, the sole source for any 737 MAX still built and for the aftermarket of at least 22,160 installed engines [B-1449];
- any supplier flagged on the supplier-shortages KPI, which is reported engines first.

The ladder is embed, buffer, keep above rate, requalify, fund, acquire [BX-1243, B-2330, B-2043]. Terms stay risk-share or cost-plus, never fixed price [BX-1262, BX-1304]. This cash, then KPI-gated Charleston capex, comes ahead of any buyback.

This is triggered only by an Aerospace Safety Committee durability or life-limited-part finding on the Joint Venture engine, and it would be treated as a live problem.

The case would need:
- CFM as the bridge;
- a maker funding a separate engine on its own return case;
- risk-share or cost-plus terms;
- that engine's own durability record before it joins the airplane;
- about 6-7 years of lead time (Pope, inference);
- a Board decision.

None of these is triggered today.

