recommendations.text:
The CFO's papers for 2050-2055:
- the net-cash bridge with the dividend;
- programme cash at $0, or $1.0B a year with the Re-engine;
- returns inside the 30-50% policy;
- any new buyback brought to the Board as a reserved matter, within the 18-month AGM authority, with the bridge attached.
There is to be no equity raise and no dividend cut. State support and refundable advances are upside and are never assumed in the case.

Public statement:
- open with the five pillars;
- give windows, not dates;
- say 'we intend to offer a choice of engines';
- time nothing to Boeing and say nothing on Delay Tactics.

levers_in_play:
Other moves (unpriced, read with the package):
(a) Wagner's engine-supply and fleet report to the Board's twice-yearly safety review, plus an unpriced contingency view, with no money committed, on keeping both NGSA sources healthy;
(b) the risk-sharing invitation on standard terms ('we intend to offer a choice of engines');
(c) if held, compete on A350 and A350F price and product, as with the A330neo, and record the hold as a business-case decision;
(d) secure our own supply, for our own ramp only;
(e) zero-for-zero trade advocacy;
(f) the A220-500 stays a study;
(g) Toepfer's capital-return paper, in which any buyback is a reserved Board matter;
(h) prepare the next generation on technology gates, with no programme cash and no date.

options_to_test:
5. IF LAUNCHED (only if both memos cleared it, which I do not expect):
- name and secure its engine before the vote;
- give a public window, not a date ('in the middle of the next decade');
- keep to one launch, with the A220-500 a study;
- no reactive cancellation. A 787 Re-engine launched this round would enter service in the same year, 2055, which on the board leaves widebody shares unchanged and is not 'first'.

red_lines:
Company launch test (profile §6) and the ExCo's own 2050 gate, recorded in 2045:
(1) at least $1B over Do Nothing;
(2) no 787 Re-engine on the record;
(3) a committed engine source, ready by entry into service, on the public record before the vote.
All three must hold. Today (1) fails at +0.57, (2) holds and (3) is not met. The gate is not set aside.

The Board's would-veto list, carried in full:
- a launch over a standing soft veto, or one that sets the ExCo's three-condition gate aside unresolved;
- a launch carried by an override;
- a launch Wagner fails on safety, quality, the A320neo or NGSA ramp, or a supply crunch;
- a launch with S2 named as the expected column.
The Board never vetoes a hold, 'none' or the engine code on its own.

frame.asks_cfo:
Thomas, this is the last round. The Board wants the A350 Re-engine's final test run under our own 2050 gate, and a full cash paper for 2050-2055. Please work from the brief's numbers only, in your 'financial flexibility' register [AX-0001].

(1) PROGRAMME CASH, 2050-2055, against $5.23B (EUR 4,753m at $1.10):
- hold: $0;
- Re-engine: $5B / 5 = $1.00B a year in 2050-2054 (19%), with 0 overlap and $0 strain, and the $5B booked at entry into service in 2055.
Confirm each and state pass or fail.

(2) ADDED-MOVE ROBUSTNESS TEST on the Re-engine. The item is +0.57 (S1) / -0.41 (S2), with break-even at 58% S2. I make it +0.37 / +0.33 / +0.28 / +0.23 at 20 / 25 / 30 / 35% S2. Please:
- confirm the arithmetic;
- give your result, and say whether it is a binding soft veto;
- state the override margin over hold (by my count +0.57, so no override can reach $1B);
- confirm it fails the company's $1B launch test by $0.43B (gate condition 1).

(3) HOLD ROBUSTNESS. Confirm the worst plausible column is S2 at +23.33, positive, and that a 787 Re-engine costs the hold row $0.04B.

(4) NET-CASH BRIDGE, 2050-2055, with the dividend, on both plans:
- returns inside 30-50%, with no equity raise and no dividend cut;
- any new buyback as a reserved matter, within the 18-month AGM authority and with the bridge attached;
- no refundable advances, risk-sharing or state support assumed;
- bank figures labelled as context only [AX-0102, AX-0104, AX-0022].

(5) WHAT HOLD FORGOES. Say it plainly: +0.57 in S1, about +0.33 weighted at 25% S2. Holding now closes the Re-engine for the game. Your 2045 bound (below $0.61B) is borne out.

(6) BOARD ITEMS. Confirm hold carries none and that there is no integrity flag. If you see a passing case, draft your half of the Re-engine's Board-gate line (Qualified Majority, $5B, about EUR 4.55bn).

(7) For each option, give pass or fail on each test, and say whether each veto is binding.

tests.numbers:
CFM's RISE is the only next-generation engine technology on the record. Its public status:
- flight test: not shown;
- compact-core durability: dust testing has started, but no turn-off result is shown;
- more than 20% fuel burn: a design and physics figure, not yet shown in engine test;
- noise below today's LEAP: shown as a capability.
CFM gives no RISE date and no Open Fan entry year.

On that record, no engine gate for a next-generation airframe has been passed. Preparing on technology gates, with no programme cash and no date, is the right engineering stance, and it draws no NGSA or A320neo teams.

other_moves.detail:
Wagner reports three status lines at every review:
- NGSA fleet results (in service since 2037);
- durability and deliveries of the P&W-Rolls-Royce Joint Venture engine. P&W says its record 'is still being earned', and no durability data is on the record, so none is estimated;
- CFM capacity across NGSA, the A320neo and the aftermarket, plus GTF Hot Section kit insertion (charged, no completion figure on record).
Any durability finding goes to the Board the day it is seen. The contingency view commits no money. A Joint Venture finding would leave NGSA on CFM alone. On paper, CFM's narrowbody volume is about a third of its 2026 level, but fps also draws on CFM and the aftermarket load is unknown. To keep both sources healthy: standard-terms partnership with both makers, and supply security for our own ramp only. The code is locked, so no third source can be added on this board.

This is the Board record. Of the three conditions in the ExCo's own 2050 gate (set in 2045):
(1) at least $1B over Do Nothing: fails at +0.57 (S1);
(2) no 787 Re-engine on the record: holds;
(3) a committed engine source, ready by a 2055 entry into service, on the record before the vote: not met. Rolls-Royce plans 'no new widebody programme beside the narrowbody', and an UltraFan widebody launched in 2050 would be ready only in 2056.
The hold rests on the business case and the engine. It is not a reaction to Boeing [A-0364, A-0302].

memo_weighing.cfo:
Toepfer's tests are role-derived (Low confidence). I accepted his memo in full.

Passed:
- peak cash on both plans: hold $0; Re-engine $1.00B a year, 19% of $5.23B, 0 overlap, $0 strain;
- plan robustness: hold's worst column is S2 at +23.33, and the Re-engine row's is +22.92;
- rule 6 and rule 3;
- the net-cash bridge: positive every year on both plans, returns inside 30-50%, no equity raise, no dividend cut, no buyback proposed;
- charge presentation, the capex tie-break and Board items (hold has none);
- integrity: no flag.

Failed:
- the added-move robustness test on the A350 Re-engine. It is the same ground as the company's $1B launch test and gate condition 1. The item is +0.57 / -0.41: $0.43B short in S1 and negative in S2.

This is a binding soft veto, not a red line and not a pillars breach. I respected it and did not override: the margin over hold is +0.57, under $1B, and it trails hold in the worst plausible column. I adopted his arithmetic: break-even at 58% S2 and +0.23 to +0.37 weighted. I also took his bridge, labelled as a FY2025-floor view with no bank figures for 2050.

