recommendations.text:
Fleet first, and durability proven in service. Boeing says in public that the Joint Venture engine is one source, that the fps falls back to CFM alone if it stumbles, and that any durability finding is a live problem. The Governance and Public Policy Committee should therefore receive, and the ExCo should state in the rationale:
- fleet hours by airframe and environment against the ~1M-hour benchmark;
- removals and time on wing against plan, and fix campaigns;
- AOG counts on both fleets, and the pacing-parts count;
- Hot Section kit insertion progress;
- the outcome of the records look-back on the 2040 capacity step. Our last escape came from capacity added for a ramp, and the powder-metal cause was found only after a records review.
AOG recovery keeps first call on parts. Fix forward with upgrades, not claims. If Eddy has no data, he should say so.

Capital in the Board's order. With no new R&D on this grid, Mitchill should show four things:
- the dividend covered and growing with earnings;
- debt at, or back to, pre-ASR levels;
- buybacks as the fill-in;
- any other use of cash coming through Finance with a return case against a buyback.
The analyst baseline stops at 2030E, so he should say where his numbers rest on the board alone. Lifecycle value ($14.7B against $9.7B nominal in 2050) is engine price x 1.6, not free cash flow; present it that way. The Board's one-round stretch stays unused.

