- the retrofit rule.
For any widebody item, his ready year against a Re-engine EIS and his support plan. A 2050 upgrade counts from 2053. UltraFan WB would be ready in 2056, a year after a 2050 Re-engine's 2055 EIS.

(5) Both veto checks, with every red-line flag struck or rebutted in `rationale`, and `overrides` empty.

(6) Any premium in `premium_b`, item by item, with its reason. At hold we expect $0.

(7) Control's metrics for the submitted plan in every column:
- own narrowbody money: $5.7B, final;
- strain: $0;
- RR widebody share in 2050: 55.2 / 55.2 / 58.0;
- RR narrowbody share in 2050: 36.5;
- Airbus widebody airframe share in 2050: 41.2 in every column.

(8) The Joint Venture governance note we asked for before this round:
- design authority, and our technology role stated plainly;
- data access to the hot section and life-limited parts;
- retrofit liability, with cost following design authority;
- work and revenue share against equal 36.5% shares;
- the joint support network;
- confirmation that our cash stays within the $4B share.

(9) The public statement, checked against the disclosure test: no date for any new programme, retrofit or kit; nothing on Re-engines or airframer dates or rates; no Joint Venture terms beyond the record.

would_veto:
