recommendations.text:
The installed fleet comes first. Boeing now says in public that the Joint Venture engine is one of exactly two fps sources, and that any durability finding is a live problem that would make it want a third source. Our position on both new narrowbodies therefore rests on durability in service, not on the next programme.

Watson should keep his quarterly report to the Safety, Energy Transition & Tech Committee. It should cover:
- durability in severe-environment operation;
- life-limited parts at full declared life;
- partner-shop induction slots and spares ahead of the first shop-visit wave from the 2037-40 cohorts;
- spare engines for severe-environment operators.

If durability bites, the Committee hears first, the fleet is protected and retrofitted at shop visit, as with the Trent 1000 package, and anything new waits. Product safety is and always must be our most important priority.

Bring the Joint Venture governance note in this package. It was due before the 2050 round. It should state plainly that on the record Pratt & Whitney holds design authority over the gear, the core and GTF2, and that we bring risk, investment and the support network. It should also ask for:
- hot-section and life-limited-part data in proportion to the risk we carry;
- cost on any retrofit to follow design authority, or a joint say in the fix;
- work and revenue share against the equal 36.5% shares.

If our content per engine is below half, McCabe should say what that does to the board's engine value. Pratt & Whitney has said in public that UltraFan NB content or a merged derivative would be a new appropriation with its own durability record. Any such proposal from us comes to this board as a new programme, on its own return case. No Rolls-Royce money beyond our $4B share goes in without the board. We left IAE for want of alignment, and we will not drift there again.

Close the game on Control's terms, stated plainly in `objective_note`:
- narrowbody entry: met (36.5% in 2045 and 2050);
- widebody dominance: met (worst year 55.2%);
- strain: met at $0, held there to the end by the two widebody holds;
- own narrowbody money: not met, and final, at $5.71B against $4B. That is the $4B share plus $1.71B written off at the 2035 fold, caused by the 2030 double selection.

We never cancel or fold for a metric. When we commit, we deliver, and we did. Keep the internal metric out of the public statement.

Public statement and other moves. Repeat what is on the record: the Joint Venture formed, its engine is in service on both new narrowbodies, and the fleet comes first. Say:
- no date for any new programme, retrofit, kit or growth engine;
- nothing on Re-engines or any airframer's dates or rates;
- no Joint Venture terms beyond the record;
- no claim of Rolls-Royce module technology in the in-service engine, which would contradict our partner's public line.

Keep both airframers on standard commercial terms through the Investment Committee, with no concessions to hold a position. On widebody, the line stays 'no new widebody programme beside the narrowbody, and Trent time on wing keeps improving'. We won't do anything not profitable.

levers_in_play:
Other moves (unpriced, read with the package):
- fleet first on the Joint Venture engine in service;
- Watson's quarterly report to the Safety, Energy Transition & Tech Committee;
- the Joint Venture governance note, delivered now;
- McCabe's quarterly funding report;
- Trent time on wing and fleet support inside the existing budget (not the `t1000_upgrade` order, no strain);
- standard commercial terms with both airframers through the Investment Committee.

