Disclosure [R-0970, R-1476, R-1011]:
- no date for any new programme, retrofit, kit or growth engine;
- nothing on Re-engines or on any airframer's dates or rates;
- no Joint Venture terms beyond the record;
- no claim of Rolls-Royce module technology in the in-service engine, which would contradict our partner's public line.

Partnership money [R-1010, R-0318]:
- no Rolls-Royce money beyond our $4B Joint Venture share without the Board;
- any UltraFan NB content or merged derivative comes to the Board as a new programme, on its own return case and its own durability record.

Control's terms, stated plainly [RG-0033, R-1561]:
- own narrowbody money is $5.71B against $4B, final, caused by the 2030 double selection;
- we never cancel or fold for a metric;
- the figure stays out of the public statement.

Balance sheet [RG-0041, RX-0261, R-0900]:
- no equity, leverage below about 1.5x, no borrowing for distributions;
- if the ramp or a fleet fix runs over, buybacks give way first;
- a fleet fix is never re-phased for cash.

tests.numbers:
At 1.36 $/£:
- Hold: £0 of new development capex. The $4B Joint Venture share and the $1.71B Solo write-off are sunk.
- Upgrade: $2B over 3 years, about $0.67B or £0.49bn a year.
- UltraFan WB: $4B over 6 years, about $0.67B or £0.49bn a year.
- Scaled against the 2026 analyst baseline, £0.49bn a year is:
  - 13-14% of FY2026 FCF guidance (£3.6-3.8bn);
  - about 12% of 2026E FCF (MS £4.2bn, GS £4.0bn);
  - 6-7% of 2030E FCF (MS £7.6bn, GS £6.9bn);
  - 5% of GS sustainable FCF in 2036E (£9.3bn);
  - 83% of 2026E and 53% of 2030E company-funded R&D (£0.59bn and £0.93bn).
- Inference: the models end in 2036E, well before 2050.

other_moves.detail:
Covers our share of the fleet: about 17,533 engines by end-2049 and 18,993 by end-2050, at 1,460 a year since 2040. The first shop-visit wave is the 2037-40 cohorts: 965 + 978 + 990 + 1,460 = 4,393 engines in our share.
Each quarter Watson reports:
- durability in severe-environment operation;
- life-limited parts at full declared life;
- partner-shop induction slots ahead of the first shop-visit wave;
- life-limited-part and hot-section spares (P&W holds design authority and controls that supply);
- spare engines for severe-environment operators;
- what we can and cannot see: hot-section and life-limited-part data sit with P&W until the governance note opens them to us.
Retrofit rule if durability bites, on the Trent 1000 model:
(1) the Committee hears first, before anything is said in public;
(2) protect the fleet with inspection intervals and a spare-engine pool;
(3) the design authority issues the kit, inserted at shop visit through the joint partner shops;
(4) anything new waits;
(5) the cost split follows the governance note, and McCabe brings a fresh number to the Board.
New-engine production does not take parts from fleet recovery. Watson claims no data, shop count or slot number the record cannot show.

